From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: nvmem on direct memory

nvmem has been off here, so every emulated SH4 memory access goes through addrspace::readt's
handler table. It is off because virtmem::init's first act fails:

    mem_region_reserve(NULL, 0x28820000)
      -> mmap(NULL, 648.125 MiB, PROT_NONE, MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON, -1, 0)

⚠ AND THE FLAGS WORD IS CORRECT, WHICH COST A WHOLE INVESTIGATION TO ESTABLISH. An earlier reading
said MAP_ANON was musl's Linux 0x20 here. It is not: $TC/include/sys/mman.h carries musl's values
and then ends with `#include <bits/mman.h>`, which #undefs them and substitutes FreeBSD's, so
MAP_ANON is 0x1000 and 0x1002 is exactly right. orbis-compat/src/orbis_mmap.cpp:54 has a standing
static_assert to the same effect.

The kernel refuses it on the POOL. Anonymous memory is FLEXIBLE memory - a per-process budget
measured at about 417 MiB at the first instruction of boot, shared with everything musl's malloc
has ever grown into - and this asks for 648 MiB of it. Direct memory had 4 601 856 KiB idle at the
same instant.

⚠ AND TEACHING mmap TO ANSWER WOULD FIX HALF A PROBLEM. Step two is create_mappings, which maps
ONE backing at SEVERAL fixed addresses: that is what the SH4's RAM mirrors are. This SDK has no
shm_open and no memfd_create, so there is no object to map twice, and beetle-psx-libretro's
Lightrec backing left the sentence for it - mmap has no way to say "the same physical pages again,
over there". Sony's allocator does; repeated sceKernelMapDirectMemory with one phys offset has
been measured on this console giving eight simultaneous coherent views.

⚠ THE COMMITTED MEMORY IS SMALL AND ONLY THE ADDRESS SPACE IS LARGE, which is the shape of the
fix. 88 MiB of backing, 128 MiB of FPCB jump table, and 512 MiB of holes that must FAULT - because
a hole is how a fastmem access to a hardware register becomes a call to a handler.

    sceKernelReserveVirtualRange   the 648 MiB, with an alignment ladder - see below
    sceKernelAllocateDirectMemory  one 88 MiB physical range, the thing the mirrors are views of
    sceKernelMapDirectMemory       MAP_FIXED, phys+offset, once per mirror
    mprotect                       region_lock/unlock, rounded to 16 KiB rather than 4096

⚠ THE ALIGNMENT CONTRACT OF sceKernelReserveVirtualRange IS IN NO ORACLE THIS PORT HAS, so it is
measured rather than assumed. The prototype is real ($TC/include/orbis/libkernel.h:379, four
parameters, no names) and the symbol is real (llvm-nm finds it defined in libkernel.so at 0xeb44).
What `align` accepts is nowhere: three separate SDK copies on this workstation declare it unnamed,
unemups4's syscall-metadata dump records four unnamed arguments, and the FreeBSD 9 oracles,
mesa-ps4 and orbis-compat have between them never called it once. So the code asks at 16 KiB,
64 KiB, 2 MiB and 0 in turn and logs which one the kernel took. One run turns the unknown into a
number; a wrong guess costs a log line rather than a session.

⚠ THE FPCB IS COMMITTED WHOLE, WHICH UPSTREAM DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO. Every other platform
leaves the 128 MiB jump table PROT_NONE and pages it in on fault. Direct memory has no such state
- sceKernelMapDirectMemory COMMITS EAGERLY - so it is paid for up front. This is not new spending:
with nvmem off the same table is a 128 MiB malloc out of the 417 MiB flexible budget, so this
HANDS 128 MiB BACK to the frontend's heap and takes it from a 4.39 GiB pool instead. bm_reset is
patched to refill directly, which is the call it already makes today.

⚠ AND THE MIRRORS ARE PROVEN INSIDE init(), because create_mappings cannot fail safely. It
verify()s every mirror, at content load, long after ram_base has been published - so a refusal
there is die() in a core that was working, rather than a fallback. init() therefore maps one
granule of the backing at two of the four Area 3 addresses, writes through one view and reads
through the other, both ways, before it says yes.

Every failure path returns false and leaves *vmem_base_addr untouched. addrspace::reserve()
ignores the return value and reads ram_base, so false means ram_base stays null, initMappings()
takes its "nvmem is DISABLED" branch, and the core behaves exactly as it did before this patch.
That is a core at 46-48 fps, and it is the floor this patch is not allowed to fall below.

Requires patch 0004: without the fastmem rewrite the first dynamic access to a hardware register
is a fatal SIGSEGV rather than a one-time call-site fixup.

diff --git a/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp b/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp
--- a/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp
+++ b/core/linux/posix_vmem.cpp
@@ -21,11 +21,85 @@
 #endif
 
 #ifdef __ORBIS__
+#include <orbis/libkernel.h>
+
 /* Defined in RetroArch/ps4/orbis_exec_mem.c, which the port's build harness links into every
  * module. Declared rather than included because that file belongs to the frontend's tree and
  * this one is upstream's - the patch stays a patch. */
 extern "C" int orbis_exec_mem_promote(void *addr, size_t len);
-#endif
+
+/* ⚠ NONE OF THE nvmem WORK BELOW GOES THROUGH mmap, AND THE REASON IS NOT THE FLAGS WORD.
+ *
+ * An earlier reading of this file blamed MAP_ANON: the SDK's <sys/mman.h> carries musl's Linux
+ * values, which would make MAP_PRIVATE|MAP_ANON 0x0022. It does not. That header ends with
+ * `#include <bits/mman.h>`, which #undefs them and substitutes FreeBSD's, so MAP_ANON is 0x1000
+ * and the shipped binary really does call mmap with 0x1002 - the correct number. The kernel
+ * refuses it anyway: anonymous memory is FLEXIBLE memory, a per-process budget measured at about
+ * 417 MiB at the first instruction of boot, and mem_region_reserve asks for 648.125 MiB of it.
+ *
+ * ⚠ AND EVEN IF IT FITTED, STEP TWO HAS NO POSIX FORM HERE. create_mappings maps ONE backing at
+ * SEVERAL fixed addresses - that is what the SH4's RAM mirrors are - and this SDK has no shm_open
+ * and no memfd_create, so there is no object to map twice. beetle-psx-libretro's Lightrec backing
+ * hit exactly this and left the sentence worth repeating: mmap has no way to say "the same
+ * physical pages again, over there". Sony's allocator does, and repeated
+ * sceKernelMapDirectMemory with one phys offset has been measured on this console giving eight
+ * simultaneous coherent views. So this file talks to it directly.
+ *
+ * ⚠ THE COMMITTED MEMORY IS SMALL AND ONLY THE ADDRESS SPACE IS LARGE, which is the whole shape
+ * of the fix. The backing is RAM+VRAM+ARAM+ERAM at their maxima - 88 MiB - and the Sh4RCB is
+ * 128 MiB of jump table. The other 512 MiB is holes, and they must FAULT, because a hole is how a
+ * fastmem access to a hardware register turns into a call to a handler. */
+namespace {
+
+/* Direct memory is handed out in 16 KiB units whatever sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE) says - and it says
+ * 4096 on this console, which is why core/stdclass.h's PAGE_SIZE is left alone and every size and
+ * address here is rounded locally instead. */
+constexpr size_t  ORBIS_GRANULE   = 0x4000;
+/* FreeBSD's MAP_FIXED, which is what Sony's mapper takes: place this mapping AT the address
+ * given. Spelled out because the SDK headers do not name it; production use of the same constant
+ * is mesa-ps4's ac_orbis_drm.c, every time a buffer changes bus. */
+constexpr int32_t ORBIS_MAP_FIXED = 0x0010;
+
+constexpr int32_t ORBIS_PROT_NONE = ORBIS_KERNEL_PROT_CPU_NONE;
+constexpr int32_t ORBIS_PROT_R    = ORBIS_KERNEL_PROT_CPU_READ;
+constexpr int32_t ORBIS_PROT_RW   = ORBIS_KERNEL_PROT_CPU_RW;
+
+inline size_t    orbisUp(size_t n)      { return (n + ORBIS_GRANULE - 1) & ~(ORBIS_GRANULE - 1); }
+inline uintptr_t orbisDown(uintptr_t p) { return p & ~(uintptr_t)(ORBIS_GRANULE - 1); }
+
+/* Sony returns 0x8002xxxx where the low half is an errno, and two of them mean opposite things:
+ * EACCES says the kernel understood the request and declined it on policy, EINVAL says the
+ * request was malformed and no policy was ever consulted. Telling them apart is the difference
+ * between "this console will not do that" and "we asked wrong". */
+const char *orbisErr(int32_t rc)
+{
+	switch ((uint32_t)rc)
+	{
+	case 0x80020001u: return "EPERM - not permitted";
+	case 0x80020009u: return "EBADF - the flags word was not anonymous";
+	case 0x8002000Cu: return "ENOMEM - out of memory or address space";
+	case 0x8002000Du: return "EACCES - understood and refused on policy";
+	case 0x8002000Eu: return "EFAULT - bad address argument";
+	case 0x80020016u: return "EINVAL - malformed request, policy never reached";
+	default:          return "not in this file's table";
+	}
+}
+
+/* The 88 MiB the mirrors are views OF. One physical range; every mirror is that same range mapped
+ * again at another address. */
+off_t  orbisBackingPhys = -1;
+size_t orbisBackingSize;
+/* The Sh4RCB: 128 MiB of FPCB jump table plus 64 KiB of SH4 context. Only on the reservation
+ * path - on the arena path these pages are part of the arena and have no handle of their own. */
+off_t  orbisRcbPhys     = -1;
+size_t orbisRcbSize;
+/* The fallback: 648 MiB of real physical memory standing in for a reservation. -1 unless the
+ * reservation path was refused. */
+off_t  orbisArenaPhys   = -1;
+size_t orbisArenaSize;
+
+} // namespace
+#endif // __ORBIS__
 
 #ifdef __ANDROID__
 #include <linux/ashmem.h>
@@ -61,21 +135,62 @@
 namespace virtmem
 {
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+/* ⚠ THE GRANULE IS 16 KiB AND THE ROUNDING GOES OUTWARDS AT BOTH ENDS.
+ *
+ * Upstream rounds the START down by PAGE_MASK and leaves the end alone, which is right when
+ * PAGE_SIZE is the kernel's unit. It is not here: sysconf(_SC_PAGESIZE) answers 4096 - which is
+ * why core/stdclass.h's PAGE_SIZE is deliberately left at 4096, since common_linux_setup()
+ * verify()s that the two agree and would abort the core if they did not - while mprotect on this
+ * kernel works in 16 KiB units and rounds outwards on its own. The range mprotect actually
+ * changes is therefore already wider than the one asked for; doing the rounding here makes the
+ * caller's arithmetic match what happens rather than widening anything further.
+ *
+ * ⚠ AND IT IS STILL mprotect() RATHER THAN sceKernelMprotect. These two functions are also called
+ * with nvmem OFF, on ordinary malloc'd arrays, in a core that works today - so the call already
+ * known to work on those pages is the one that stays. sceKernelMprotect is used where this port
+ * has measured it to be necessary (promoting pages to executable), and this is not that. */
+static bool orbis_protect(void *start, size_t len, int prot, const char *what)
+{
+	const uintptr_t lo = orbisDown((uintptr_t)start);
+	const uintptr_t hi = (uintptr_t)orbisUp((size_t)((uintptr_t)start + len));
+	if (mprotect((void *)lo, (size_t)(hi - lo), prot))
+	{
+		ERROR_LOG(VMEM, "region_%s(%p, %zu) -> mprotect(%p, %zu) failed, errno %d",
+				what, start, len, (void *)lo, (size_t)(hi - lo), errno);
+		return false;
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+#endif
+
 bool region_lock(void *start, size_t len)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	if (!orbis_protect(start, len, PROT_READ, "lock"))
+		die("mprotect failed...");
+	return true;
+#else
 	size_t inpage = (uintptr_t)start & PAGE_MASK;
 	if (mprotect((u8*)start - inpage, len + inpage, PROT_READ))
 		die("mprotect failed...");
 	return true;
+#endif
 }
 
 bool region_unlock(void *start, size_t len)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	if (!orbis_protect(start, len, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE, "unlock"))
+		die("mprotect  failed...");
+	return true;
+#else
 	size_t inpage = (uintptr_t)start & PAGE_MASK;
 	if (mprotect((u8*)start - inpage, len + inpage, PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE))
 		// Add some way to see why it failed? gdb> info proc mappings
 		die("mprotect  failed...");
 	return true;
+#endif
 }
 
 bool region_set_exec(void *start, size_t len)
@@ -93,8 +208,111 @@
 	return true;
 }
 
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+/* ⚠ THE ALIGNMENT CONTRACT OF sceKernelReserveVirtualRange IS NOT IN THIS PORT'S ORACLES, SO IT
+ * IS ESTABLISHED HERE RATHER THAN ASSUMED.
+ *
+ * What IS established: the prototype, from the SDK's own <orbis/libkernel.h>:379,
+ *
+ *     int32_t sceKernelReserveVirtualRange(void **addr, size_t len, int32_t flags, size_t align);
+ *
+ * and that the symbol is real - llvm-nm finds it defined in the SDK's libkernel.so stub at
+ * 0xeb44, beside sceKernelMapDirectMemory and sceKernelMprotect, both of which this port already
+ * calls in production. What is NOT established anywhere is what the kernel will accept in
+ * `align`: three separate SDK copies on this workstation declare the four parameters with no
+ * names at all, the syscall-metadata dump that ships with unemups4 records it as four unnamed
+ * arguments, and the workshop's FreeBSD 9 oracles, mesa-ps4 and orbis-compat have between them
+ * never called it once.
+ *
+ * So it is measured instead of guessed: the ladder below asks for the same range at each
+ * plausible alignment and the log says which one answered. One run on hardware turns an unknown
+ * into a written-down number, and a wrong guess costs a line in the log rather than a session.
+ * Smallest first, because a coarser alignment than the kernel needs is never wrong - it only
+ * wastes address space, of which there is 128 TiB.
+ *
+ * ⚠ AND ZERO IS IN THE LADDER ON PURPOSE, LAST. If the kernel reads 0 as "no constraint" it will
+ * answer; if it reads 0 as malformed it returns EINVAL and costs nothing. Putting it first would
+ * hide which real alignment works, and that is the number worth learning. */
+static void *orbis_reserve_range(size_t len)
+{
+	static const size_t ladder[] = { ORBIS_GRANULE, 0x10000, 0x200000, 0 };
+
+	for (size_t align : ladder)
+	{
+		void *at = nullptr;
+		const int32_t rc = sceKernelReserveVirtualRange(&at, len, 0, align);
+		if (rc == 0 && at != nullptr)
+		{
+			NOTICE_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: sceKernelReserveVirtualRange(%zu MiB, align 0x%zx) -> %p. "
+					"THAT ALIGNMENT IS THE ANSWER - write it down, it was in no oracle.",
+					len / (1024 * 1024), align, at);
+			return at;
+		}
+		WARN_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: sceKernelReserveVirtualRange(%zu MiB, align 0x%zx) -> 0x%08x (%s)",
+				len / (1024 * 1024), align, (unsigned)rc, orbisErr(rc));
+	}
+
+	/* ⚠ THE FALLBACK COSTS 648 MiB OF REAL PHYSICAL MEMORY AND IS HERE ANYWAY, because every call
+	 * in it has already run on this console. mesa-ps4 does exactly this for the graphics arena -
+	 * one direct-memory range mapped once, then over-mapped piecewise with MAP_FIXED - and its
+	 * own comment calls the result "an address-space reservation whose pages can be replaced".
+	 * Direct memory measured 4 601 856 KiB idle at boot, so 648 MiB is affordable; it is simply
+	 * wasteful, which is why it is second and says so.
+	 *
+	 * ⚠ AND IT GOES PROT_NONE IMMEDIATELY, WHICH IS NOT TIDINESS. The holes in the 512 MiB window
+	 * are how a fastmem access to a hardware register becomes a fault and then a call to a
+	 * handler. An arena left readable would answer those accesses with RAM, and the emulator
+	 * would be silently wrong rather than loudly slow. */
+	orbisArenaSize = orbisUp(len);
+	int32_t rc = sceKernelAllocateDirectMemory(0, sceKernelGetDirectMemorySize(), orbisArenaSize,
+			ORBIS_GRANULE, ORBIS_KERNEL_WB_ONION, &orbisArenaPhys);
+	if (rc != 0)
+	{
+		orbisArenaPhys = -1;
+		WARN_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: no reservation and no %zu MiB arena either -> 0x%08x (%s)",
+				orbisArenaSize / (1024 * 1024), (unsigned)rc, orbisErr(rc));
+		return nullptr;
+	}
+
+	void *at = nullptr;
+	rc = sceKernelMapDirectMemory(&at, orbisArenaSize, ORBIS_PROT_RW, 0, orbisArenaPhys,
+			ORBIS_GRANULE);
+	if (rc != 0 || at == nullptr)
+	{
+		WARN_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: could not map the %zu MiB arena -> 0x%08x (%s)",
+				orbisArenaSize / (1024 * 1024), (unsigned)rc, orbisErr(rc));
+		sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(orbisArenaPhys, orbisArenaSize);
+		orbisArenaPhys = -1;
+		return nullptr;
+	}
+
+	rc = sceKernelMprotect(at, orbisArenaSize, ORBIS_PROT_NONE);
+	if (rc != 0)
+	{
+		ERROR_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: the arena at %p would not go PROT_NONE -> 0x%08x (%s). Refusing "
+				"it: holes that answer instead of faulting would make every hardware register "
+				"read return RAM.", at, (unsigned)rc, orbisErr(rc));
+		sceKernelMunmap(at, orbisArenaSize);
+		sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(orbisArenaPhys, orbisArenaSize);
+		orbisArenaPhys = -1;
+		return nullptr;
+	}
+
+	NOTICE_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: no virtual-range reservation on this kernel - standing in a %zu MiB "
+			"direct-memory arena at %p instead. This WORKS and it is expensive; if the ladder "
+			"above ever answers, this branch stops being taken.",
+			orbisArenaSize / (1024 * 1024), at);
+	return at;
+}
+#endif
+
 static void *mem_region_reserve(void *start, size_t len)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	/* flycast only ever asks for a kernel-chosen address here. */
+	verify(start == nullptr);
+	return orbis_reserve_range(len);
+#else
 	void *p = mmap(start, len, PROT_NONE, MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANON, -1, 0);
 	if (p == MAP_FAILED)
 	{
@@ -103,15 +321,68 @@
 	}
 	else
 		return p;
+#endif
 }
 
 static bool mem_region_release(void *start, size_t len)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	/* ⚠ THE MAPPING AND THE PHYSICAL PAGES ARE TWO DIFFERENT HANDLES AND BOTH HAVE TO GO BACK.
+	 * This port has already written down that direct memory is NOT reclaimed when a mapping goes
+	 * away, and this runs on every content unload - so anything leaked here leaks per load, on a
+	 * console with no swap. sceKernelMunmap takes the address, sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory takes
+	 * the offset the allocator returned, and a caller holding only the first cannot give the
+	 * memory back. */
+	const bool ok = sceKernelMunmap(start, len) == 0;
+	if (orbisRcbPhys >= 0)
+	{
+		sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(orbisRcbPhys, orbisRcbSize);
+		orbisRcbPhys = -1;
+	}
+	if (orbisArenaPhys >= 0)
+	{
+		sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(orbisArenaPhys, orbisArenaSize);
+		orbisArenaPhys = -1;
+	}
+	return ok;
+#else
 	return munmap(start, len) == 0;
+#endif
 }
 
 static void *mem_region_map_file(void *file_handle, void *dest, size_t len, size_t offset, bool readwrite)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	/* ⚠ THE "FILE" IS A PHYSICAL OFFSET, WHICH IS THE ENTIRE POINT OF THIS BACKEND. There is no
+	 * fd because there is no shm_open on this SDK; what create_mappings wants is one object at
+	 * several addresses, and sceKernelMapDirectMemory called repeatedly with the same phys is the
+	 * form of that request the kernel does answer. */
+	(void)file_handle;
+	if (dest == nullptr || orbisBackingPhys < 0)
+		return nullptr;
+	/* Every one of these comes out of addrspace.cpp's mem_mappings table, whose starts and sizes
+	 * are multiples of 8 MiB - but a table is a thing that gets edited, and a MAP_FIXED landing
+	 * where the kernel rounded it rather than where the emulator meant it would be a wrong mirror
+	 * rather than an error. */
+	if ((((uintptr_t)dest | len | offset) & (ORBIS_GRANULE - 1)) != 0
+			|| offset + len > orbisBackingSize)
+	{
+		ERROR_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: mirror at %p, %zu bytes from backing offset %zu is not a 16 KiB "
+				"multiple or runs past the %zu-byte backing", dest, len, offset, orbisBackingSize);
+		return nullptr;
+	}
+
+	void *at = dest;
+	const int32_t rc = sceKernelMapDirectMemory(&at, len, readwrite ? ORBIS_PROT_RW : ORBIS_PROT_R,
+			ORBIS_MAP_FIXED, orbisBackingPhys + (off_t)offset, ORBIS_GRANULE);
+	if (rc != 0 || at != dest)
+	{
+		ERROR_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: mirror of backing+%zu (%zu KiB) at %p -> 0x%08x (%s), landed at %p",
+				offset, len / 1024, dest, (unsigned)rc, orbisErr(rc), at);
+		return nullptr;
+	}
+	return at;
+#else
 	int flags = MAP_SHARED | MAP_NOSYNC | (dest != NULL ? MAP_FIXED : 0);
 	void *p = mmap(dest, len, PROT_READ | (readwrite ? PROT_WRITE : 0), flags, (int)(uintptr_t)file_handle, offset);
 	if (p == MAP_FAILED)
@@ -121,6 +392,7 @@
 	}
 	else
 		return p;
+#endif
 }
 
 // Allocates memory via a fd on shmem/ahmem or even a file on disk
@@ -170,6 +442,153 @@
 // memory using a fallback (that is, regular mallocs and falling back to slow memory JIT).
 bool init(void **vmem_base_addr, void **sh4rcb_addr, size_t ramSize)
 {
+#ifdef __ORBIS__
+	// ⚠ EVERY FAILURE BELOW RETURNS false AND LEAVES *vmem_base_addr UNTOUCHED, WHICH IS THE
+	// WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT FOR THIS BACKEND. addrspace::reserve() ignores the return value and
+	// reads ram_base, so a false here means ram_base stays null, addrspace::initMappings() takes
+	// its "nvmem is DISABLED" branch, and the core behaves exactly as it did before this patch
+	// existed - which is a core that runs. Nothing here is allowed to half-succeed.
+	orbisBackingSize = orbisUp(ramSize);
+	int32_t rc = sceKernelAllocateDirectMemory(0, sceKernelGetDirectMemorySize(), orbisBackingSize,
+			ORBIS_GRANULE, ORBIS_KERNEL_WB_ONION, &orbisBackingPhys);
+	if (rc != 0)
+	{
+		orbisBackingPhys = -1;
+		WARN_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: no %zu MiB of direct memory for the emulated machine's RAM -> "
+				"0x%08x (%s) - falling back to handler tables",
+				orbisBackingSize / (1024 * 1024), (unsigned)rc, orbisErr(rc));
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	reserved_size = 512_MB + sizeof(Sh4RCB) + ARAM_SIZE_MAX + 0x10000;
+	reserved_base = mem_region_reserve(NULL, reserved_size);
+	if (reserved_base == nullptr)
+	{
+		sceKernelReleaseDirectMemory(orbisBackingPhys, orbisBackingSize);
+		orbisBackingPhys = -1;
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	uintptr_t ptrint = ((uintptr_t)reserved_base + 0x10000 - 1) & (~(uintptr_t)0xffff);
+
+	// ⚠ THE FPCB IS COMMITTED WHOLE HERE, AND UPSTREAM DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO THAT.
+	//
+	// Every other platform leaves the 128 MiB jump table PROT_NONE and pages it in on fault - the
+	// region_unlock further down covers only `sizeof(Sh4RCB) - fpcb_size`, the 64 KiB of SH4
+	// context. Demand paging needs pages that exist and are merely forbidden. Direct memory has
+	// no such state: sceKernelMapDirectMemory COMMITS EAGERLY, so "reserved but not yet backed"
+	// is not something this allocator can express, and a fault on an unbacked address inside the
+	// reservation could only be answered by allocating and mapping a granule from inside the
+	// signal handler - 8192 separate physical carve-outs in the worst case, on a console where
+	// direct memory is not reclaimed when a mapping goes away.
+	//
+	// So it is paid for up front instead. That is 128 MiB of direct memory, out of the 4 601 856
+	// KiB measured idle at boot - and it is not even new spending: with nvmem off, the same table
+	// is a 128 MiB malloc, which comes out of FLEXIBLE memory, of which this process has about
+	// 417 MiB in total. Committing it here HANDS 128 MiB BACK to the frontend's heap.
+	//
+	// The consequence is one behaviour change, and hw/mem/addrspace.cpp's bm_reset is patched to
+	// match: reset_mem can no longer make these pages fault again, so the refill a fault would
+	// have done is done directly. That is what the nvmem-off path already does today.
+	orbisRcbSize = orbisUp(sizeof(Sh4RCB));
+	if (orbisArenaPhys >= 0)
+	{
+		// The arena path already has physical pages here; they are only PROT_NONE.
+		rc = sceKernelMprotect((void *)ptrint, orbisRcbSize, ORBIS_PROT_RW);
+		if (rc != 0)
+		{
+			ERROR_LOG(VMEM, "nvmem: the Sh4RCB at %p would not go read-write inside the arena -> "
